---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { release, url } from '../lib/publication';
const claim = (id: string) => release.claims.find(c => c.id === id);
const source = (id: string) => {
  const item = claim(id);
  return item ? url(`/sources/${item.evidenceIds[0]}/`) : url('/sources/');
};
const current = claim('C-IMD-RAINFALL-DEP-imd-south-peninsular-20260923');
const y2002 = claim('C-IMD-HISTORY-SOUTH-2002');
const y2009 = claim('C-IMD-HISTORY-SOUTH-2009');
const y2025 = claim('C-IMD-HISTORY-SOUTH-2025');
---
<Site title="When was it last this bad?" description="Three source-linked South Peninsula monsoon seasons and why the partial 2026 figure cannot yet be ranked against them.">
  <PageHeader title="When was it last this bad?" eyebrow="History / same season, different year" description="The honest answer is not a year. It is a rule: compare the same place, the same months and the same cutoff—or refuse the rank." />
  <section class="section g11-chapter"><div class="shell"><div class="g11-chapter-head"><span class="g11-chapter-number">01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">The trap in the comparison</p><h2>Seven September days separate the questions.</h2></div></div>
    <div class="grid-2"><div><span class="g11-tag measured">Measured · IMD reported</span><p class="g11-pull">For South Peninsular India, IMD reported <strong>−25.7%</strong> through <strong>23 September 2026</strong>. <a href={current ? source(current.id) : url('/sources/')}>2026 regional graphic ↗</a></p></div><div class="g11-clock"><span class="g11-tag history">Historical context</span><p>Our reviewed older rows cover <strong>every day from June through 30 September</strong>. Seven further days of rain can change a seasonal total. A partial 2026 departure cannot be ranked among complete seasons. The regional graphic also omits each cumulative normal total, so the 2026 regional percentage is attributed to IMD.</p></div></div>
    <p class="small muted">The IMD South Peninsula here is a ten-subdivision rainfall region, not a selected set of current states or the CWC southern reservoir cohort. <a href={url('/methodology/')}>Comparison rules ↗</a></p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section g11-chapter"><div class="shell"><div class="g11-chapter-head"><span class="g11-chapter-number">02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">Completed seasons / one IMD series</p><h2>Three years show why context matters.</h2></div></div>
    <div class="g11-signal-grid">
      {y2002 && <article class="g11-signal"><span class="g11-tag history">Historical context</span><span>South Peninsula / June–September 2002</span><strong>−34.8%</strong><p>466.9 mm in the completed season against the table's displayed 1971–2020 normal. This is rain, not evidence of equivalent 2026 water or human impacts.</p><a href={source(y2002.id)}>IMD row and calculation ↗</a></article>}
      {y2009 && <article class="g11-signal"><span class="g11-tag history">Historical context</span><span>South Peninsula / June–September 2009</span><strong>−4.8%</strong><p>681.6 mm in the South Peninsula; all India was 709.1 mm, <strong>−18.4%</strong> against its own normal. National and southern signals differed.</p><a href={source(y2009.id)}>IMD rows and calculation ↗</a></article>}
      {y2025 && <article class="g11-signal"><span class="g11-tag history">Historical context</span><span>South Peninsula / June–September 2025</span><strong>+9.9%</strong><p>787.2 mm in the completed season. A recent wetter year counters any claim that every season followed one downward path.</p><a href={source(y2025.id)}>IMD row and calculation ↗</a></article>}
    </div>
    <p class="g11-pull">These three rows do not say which year 2026 resembles. They show why selecting one famous drought year can mislead.</p>
  </div></section>
  <section class="section g11-chapter"><div class="shell"><div class="g11-chapter-head"><span class="g11-chapter-number">03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">The next defensible comparison</p><h2>What would it take to answer the headline?</h2></div></div>
    <ol class="g11-sequence"><li><h3>Match the cutoff.</h3><p>Get a reviewed historical daily series for the same South Peninsula footprint and stop every year on 23 September—or await an approved full 2026 season.</p></li><li><h3>Keep the same measuring rules.</h3><p>Check series version, boundary, unit and normal period. Do not splice the all-India and South Peninsula rows or the CWC reservoir region.</p></li><li><h3>Test the human comparison separately.</h3><p>Storage, irrigation, service, markets and public response can change consequences even when rainfall resembles an earlier year. We have not approved episode-specific impact records here.</p></li></ol>
    <div class="g11-epilogue"><p class="eyebrow">Current answer</p><h3>No 2026 rank. No “worst since” claim.</h3><p>The approved finding is a dated rainfall departure, with a clear path to a proper comparison when like-for-like data are available.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href={url('/explore/')}>Inspect current rainfall ↗</a><a class="button button-light" href={url('/sources/')}>Inspect historical source ↗</a></div></div>
  </div></section>
</Site>
